room      law_1
kind      text
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Axioms, or Laws of Motion, Law I.
caption   Law I — Every body perseveres in its state of rest, or of uniform motion in a right line, unless it is compelled to change that state by forces impress'd thereon.
final     4

station 1
  ceiling   eq1 :: \sum \vec{F} = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \vec{v} = \text{const}
  gloss   Law I fixes rest and uniform straight-line motion as the natural states a body keeps until an impressed force changes them.
  color   rest #1B6FB3
  color   uniform #2E8B57
  color   force #B22222
  panel
    phrase  rest "a state of rest"
    phrase  uniform "uniform motion in a right line"
    phrase  force "forces impress'd thereon" heart
  text
    Every body perseveres in {rest|a state of rest}, or of {uniform|uniform motion in a right line}, \\
    unless compelled to change that state. A body left to itself does nothing on its own — it neither \\
    slows, speeds, nor turns. The sole agent that alters this perseverance is a {force|force impress'd thereon}, \\
    so that with $\sum \vec{F} = 0$ we have $\vec{v} = \text{const}$: velocity, in both speed and direction, is preserved.

station 2
  ceiling   eq2 :: \omega = \text{const} \quad (\text{air resistance} \to 0)
  gloss   The spinning top perseveres in its rotation, ceasing only as the air retards it.
  color   top #C77800
  color   cohesion #6A0DAD
  color   air #4682B4
  panel
    phrase  top "a top" heart
    phrase  cohesion "cohesion"
    phrase  air "the air"
  text
    {top|A top}, whose parts by their {cohesion|cohesion} are perpetually drawn aside from rectilinear motions, \\
    does not cease its rotation otherwise than as it is retarded by {air|the air}. Each part is forever pulled \\
    inward from the straight line it would take, yet the whole endures because no force impels it to stop. \\
    Remove the {air|air} and the top would spin forever, its angular speed unchanged, $\omega = \text{const}$.

station 3
  ceiling   eq3 :: \vec{v}_{\text{prog}},\, \vec{\omega}_{\text{orb}} = \text{const} \;\; (t \to \infty)
  gloss   Planets and comets, in nearly empty space, preserve both their progressive and circular motions for immense times.
  color   bodies #8B0000
  color   free #2E8B57
  color   motions #1B6FB3
  panel
    phrase  bodies "planets and comets" heart
    phrase  free "more free spaces"
    phrase  motions "motions both progressive and circular"
  text
    The greater bodies of the {bodies|planets and comets}, meeting with less resistance in {free|more free spaces}, \\
    preserve the {motions|motions both progressive and circular} for a much longer time. In the near-void between \\
    the stars scarcely anything retards them, so their onward drift and their orbital turning both endure. \\
    These are the law's strongest witnesses: across immense times, $\vec{v}_{\text{prog}}$ and $\vec{\omega}_{\text{orb}}$ hold nearly constant.

station 4
  ceiling   eq4 :: \vec{a} = \vec{g} + \frac{\vec{F}_{\text{air}}}{m}
  gloss   Projectiles persevere in their motions except as air resistance and gravity impress forces upon them.
  color   proj #B22222
  color   resist #4682B4
  color   gravity #6A0DAD
  panel
    phrase  proj "projectiles" heart
    phrase  resist "resistance of the air"
    phrase  gravity "force of gravity"
  text
    {proj|Projectiles} persevere in their motions, so far as they are not retarded by the {resist|resistance of the air}, \\
    or impelled downwards by the {gravity|force of gravity}. A stone or a cannonball, once flung, would fly straight \\
    on forever were these two forces absent — shot fast enough horizontally, it would circle the whole Earth. \\
    What we see as a falling arc is only the {proj|projectile's} straight perseverance bent by $\vec{a} = \vec{g} + \tfrac{\vec{F}_{\text{air}}}{m}$.
